 ## Overview
-Dominant-Axis Triangle Voxelization is a rasterization-based method for converting triangle meshes into a sparse voxel grid (such as a Sparse Voxel Octree or DAG) by projecting the triangle onto a 2D plane defined by its dominant normal axis [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently determining which voxels are intersected by a triangle while minimizing redundant depth tests [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from general 3D grid rasterization by explicitly classifying triangles into "thin" (1-voxel thick in one axis) and "thick" categories, using 2D projection tests for thin triangles and column-casting with secondary plane checks for thick ones [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Dominant-Axis Triangle Voxelization is a rasterization-based method for converting triangle meshes into a sparse voxel grid (such as a Sparse Voxel Octree or DAG) by projecting the triangle onto a 2D plane defined by its dominant normal axis [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently determining which voxels are intersected by a triangle while minimizing redundant depth tests [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from general 3D grid rasterization by explicitly classifying triangles into "thin" (1-voxel thick in one axis) and "thick" categories, using 2D projection tests for thin triangles and column-casting with secondary plane checks for thick ones [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
